.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- execution-target device for gpu(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/gpuclone
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I gpu
device is the native backend
.IR gpu (2)'s
own
.B Backend.apply
talks to when configured for
.BR "device gpu" ;
scripts and most Limbo programs should reach it through
.IR gpu (2)
rather than opening it directly.
.PP
Unlike
.IR ssl (3),
.I gpu
binds flat into
.B /dev
(matching
.IR audio (3)'s
own
.BR /dev/audio / /dev/audioctl
convention, not
.IR ssl (3)'s
own numbered-subdirectory one) \(em its one file is
.BR /dev/gpuclone ,
not a
.B clone
file under a
.B /dev/gpu
directory. Opening
.B /dev/gpuclone
allocates one handle: matrix state (the uploaded
.IR sparse (2)
CSR arrays) and the pending response live on that one open channel,
which also serves both reads and writes \(em there is no separate
.B ctl
/
.B data
split, since every message on a handle is naturally one request
immediately followed by one response.
.PP
The protocol is line-oriented text, not binary, for now (see
.B PERFORMANCE
below). Each write is one request; the following read returns its
response. Three request shapes, each starting with a one-letter tag:
.EX
	P
.EE
The following read returns
.B f32
or
.BR f64 :
the precision this device would
.B actually
compute in. Not cosmetic \(em a hardware backend is f32-only (Metal
has no double), so a caller needing f64 must know that
.B before
trusting a result. This is what
.IR gpu (2)'s
.B precision
verb queries.
.EX
	U n nnz
	rowptr (n+1 space-separated integers)
	colidx (nnz space-separated integers)
	val (nnz space-separated reals)
.EE
Uploads an
.IR n \(mu n
CSR matrix with
.I nnz
nonzeros, replacing any previous upload on this handle. The following
read returns
.B OK
on success, or an error.
.EX
	X x (n space-separated reals)
.EE
Computes
.IR y = Ax
against the uploaded matrix (an error if none has been uploaded, or
.I x
doesn't have exactly
.I n
values). The following read returns
.I y
the same way, space-separated reals.
.PP
Closing the handle frees its matrix state.
.SH IMPLEMENTATION
The numeric kernel is not this device's own job. It dispatches through
three nullable hooks \(em
.BR gpuhwupload ,
.BR gpuhwspmv ,
.B gpuhwfree
\(em exactly the convention
.I devdraw.c
already uses for
.BR gpudrawfillpoly3d / g :
a platform backend that has real GPU compute assigns them at load
time, and everywhere they are nil every matvec silently uses this
device's own portable CPU loop instead. Adding or removing a hardware
backend therefore changes nothing about this device's Styx surface,
and no platform
.B #ifdef
appears in it.
.PP
On this platform
.RB ( win-gpu.m ,
named by the
.B gpu
line in the
.B emu-cocoa
device manifest, the same way
.B draw
names
.BR win-cocoa )
those hooks are a real
.B MTLComputePipelineState
running one GPU thread per matrix row. The matrix buffers are uploaded
once per matrix and stay device-resident; only the x and y vectors
cross per matvec \(em which is the whole reason offloading a Krylov
solve pays off, and what
.IR gpu (2)'s
.B resident
verb refers to. That file deliberately includes no Inferno headers at
all: the hooks are plain C types on both sides, so it needs neither
.I dat.h
nor the
.BR Point / Rect
renaming
.I win-cocoa.m
has to do.
.PP
Metal Shading Language has no
.BR double ,
so the hardware path is inherently f32, converting on the way in and
out. That is a hardware property, not a shortcut, and it is why the
.B P
request above exists: rather than quietly returning single precision
to a caller who asked for double,
.IR gpu (2)
asks this device what it would really compute in and declines to use
it when that is not good enough. The effect is directly observable
\(em an
.IR fem (2)
Poisson solve with
.B "backend gpu precision f32"
converges in 9 CG iterations to a residual around 1e\-11, against 7
iterations and 1e\-17 for the same solve on the CPU, with the two
solutions agreeing to about 4e\-8: f32 epsilon, exactly as expected.
.PP
The text protocol remains a correctness-first choice, and is now the
obvious next thing to improve: matrix upload happens once per solve so
its parsing cost is irrelevant, but the
.B X
exchange runs every Krylov iteration and currently formats and reparses
the whole vector as text each time. A compact binary encoding there
\(em which changes neither
.IR gpu (2)'s
interface nor the hardware hooks \(em is where the real throughput win
now is, since the kernel itself already runs on the GPU.
.SH SOURCE
.B /emu/port/devgpu.c
.br
.B /emu/MacOSX/win-gpu.m
